977:# Enumeration is THREE materialized, separately status-checked stages. A single
978:# `find … | sort | xargs` reports only xargs's status, so a find that emits a
979:# valid partial stream and then dies yields a SHORT BUT CONSISTENT inventory
980:# (F2, I13; executably reproduced in §7.4f case V).
1018:# Preflight: the archive operand must exist relative to the -C directory.
1026:# Fail-closed listing assertions: the archive really carries the tree.
1027:# The listing is MATERIALIZED to a file and its status checked before anything
1028:# filters it -- `tar -tf … | grep -q` can exit 0 on a tar that failed (F3, I13).
1064:# The five exclusions, stated ONCE as anchored './x' patterns so the tar set and
1065:# the find set below cannot drift apart:
1066:#   .git / .temp / .task-board  -- not build inputs, and large.
1067:#   .claude / .codex            -- they hold the only two symlinks in the tree
1068:#     (.claude/skills/skill-go-testing-tools, .codex/skills/skill-go-testing-tools).
1069:#     Extracting a symlink on Windows needs Developer Mode or admin; excluding
1070:#     them removes the failure mode entirely and changes no Go input.
1072:# --- step 1: enumerate the INTENDED set at the origin, before any archiving.
1073:#     This inventory, not the archive, is the authority on completeness -- which
1074:#     is exactly why it may not be produced by a pipeline. `find | sort | xargs`
1075:#     reports only xargs's status, so a find that emits a valid partial stream
1076:#     and then dies yields a SHORT BUT INTERNALLY CONSISTENT inventory that every
1077:#     later check agrees with (F2, I13). Three stages, three materialized files,
1078:#     three separately compared statuses. No `pipefail`: POSIX /bin/sh has none.
1096:# --- step 2: create the archive as its own process. Check ITS status.
1103:# --- step 3 and 4: list, then extract. Each status-checked, neither piped.
1107:# --- step 5: the complete-set assertion. `-c` catches changed and MISSING files;
1108:#     the count catches EXTRA ones, which `-c` structurally cannot see.
